SM(223,{"n":"Genesis 3.1-8","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 3.1-8.mp3","d":3166.1,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.08,"We'll go ahead and get started, and when we get started, in just a moment, we're picking up in uh Genesis chapter 3."],[27.12,"Um nothing really eventful is gonna happen here."],[33.68,"Uh but uh we don't we don't have a lot to recap because we're not that far into the Bible."],[42.32,"Um So and and I won't belabor things uh too much."],[48.88,"I always belabor things a little, but not too much."],[52.24,"Um so uh I'm not gonna go through our whole big long introduction to the Torah, introduction to Genesis and all that again."],[60.76,"Uh Genesis 1, obviously, through uh 1-1 through 2-1, we read uh the uh creation of the world, structured around seven days, uh Genesis 2."],[77.5,"Uh we read about the creation of humanity, first of man, then as then of woman."],[85.82,"Um that really brings us to chapter 3."],[89.26,"Is that the quickest review ever?"],[90.94,"Yeah. Um because if I go any more into that, we'll have less time for chapter three."],[98.7,"So let's just go ahead, unless anybody has any leftover questions or comments or Christmas. goodies they want to give me"],[106.82,"We'll go ahead and uh get started in Genesis chapter 3, verse 1."],[113.14,"Now the serpent was more cunning than all the wild animals the Lord God made on the earth."],[121.46,"And he said to the woman, has God indeed said, You shall not eat from every tree of the garden."],[137.6,"Because we've already got a bunch of things just in this one verse."],[141.04,"So the first thing we have to talk about is the serpent."],[150.84,"This story is not, I know I've been referencing Rudyard Kipling a lot in the last couple of chapters, but this is not Rudyard Kipling's just so story about how the snake lost his legs."],[164.05,"So this is not snakes used to have legs like lizards until this happened."],[171.97,"And and this is also not the story of a talking snake."],[180.31,"It's not like animals used to talk in paradise, and then they just they lost that ability outside of animation."],[188.01,"Um so the word here that we translate as serpent is originally the Hebrew word nekash."],[202.77,"And one of the things you can do in Hebrew, uh all Hebrew words have what are called triliteral roots."],[211.97,"What that means is they're made up of three consonants."],[236.29,"Not all cases, but in a lot of cases, tell you what part of speech the word is."],[245.13,"And Hebrew doesn't really have tenses."],[249.45,"They have a thing called tense, but it's confusing because it has nothing to do with time."],[254.41,"But the the way to interpret the word, if it's a verb, or a noun."],[262.59,"So sometimes there are letters that get added to those three, that three consonant root."],[273.55,"But sometimes there's not."],[275.79,"And so sometimes there's this room for interpretation"],[280.33,"And Hebrew auth.ors who are writing without the vowels knew that there was this room for interpretation"],[287.09,"And so, especially in Hebrew poetry, you get a lot of wordplay that way."],[292.77,"Because you have a word which, well, if it's this, it would mean this, if it's that, it would mean that."],[298.45,"And so you could kind of refer to both, by using that particular word and leaving it a little ambiguous."],[305.98,"And so Nakash here, there are no, if you take the later vowel markings out, there are no consonantal markers, no consonants that mark what part of speech this is."],[324.34,"And so this word can mean different things."],[329.03,"So if it's a noun, this is why they're translating it as serpent."],[333.59,"If it's a noun, it refers to a serpent."],[339.91,"If it is um a uh an uh adjective, it's an adjective, then it refers to uh the uh clever or wise one."],[363.24,"If it's a participle, meaning if it's a verb being used sort of as a noun, then it means the literally the brazen one or the shining one."],[378.05,"Brazen is in bronze."],[383.98,"So it has sort of all three of those included meanings there."],[393.27,"And one of those is kind of bolstered by the way it's translated, the Orthodox Study Bible is more cunning."],[400.12,"And uh that word kind of means cunning or devious or slippery."],[405.36,"Um, I think I forget which translation is."],[407.72,"There's one English translation that has subtle."],[410.84,"Yeah. Which is an older meaning of the word subtle"],[415.84,"It's not he's the most subtle, as in like he just hints at things."],[419.06,"Um but um instead of blatant."],[422.28,"Um but that kind of idea."],[426.92,"Okay. So why is that important?"],[431.16,"Well, this is hinting at that this creature is more than just again, a snake, a talking snake."],[440.73,"Um it is a serpentine being who is shining, glowing."],[448.86,"Brazen, and is also wise or clever or devious."],[456.26,"So if we want to get helped out on who this is."],[462.12,"Because this is his introduction."],[463.4,"He just shows up here."],[465.85,"And he's not going to show up and expressly this form again."],[472.11,"After we're done with Genesis 3, we're not going to have the serpent, directly referring to this."],[483.65,"John actually makes a reference to it, but he doesn't appear as a character under this name."],[490.57,"But there are a couple of places, one in Ezekiel and one in Isaiah, where uh the figure that's going to become known as the devil is talked about."],[507.14,"One is uh an Isaiah the prophecy against the king of Tyre."],[512.56,"Which is divided into two pieces."],[515.84,"There's a prophecy against the prince of Tyre, which is talking about the human king, and if you read that, you can tell."],[523.6,"And then it switches from the prince of Tyre to the king of Tyre."],[528.82,"And in doing so, it switches from the human king to sort of the spiritual power that stands behind him, which is Baal."],[537.88,"And starts talking about Baal."],[540.48,"Um, and then in Ezekiel, there is another prophetic passage that does a similar thing."],[549.05,"And in Ezekiel, it refers to this divine figure, who is cast down out of heaven."],[557.87,"We'll get to the casting down later in this chapter."],[561.55,"Um, who is cast out of heaven."],[564.75,"It talks about him walking on the mountain of Assembly, the mountain of Eden."],[570.83,"We talked about Eden being a mountain."],[572.69,"So it talks about that figure being in Eden."],[575.57,"And there he's referred to as a guardian cherub."],[580.19,"Which is Keruv."],[580.67,"Keruv. Um we talked aboutim or Cherubim in uh Revelation"],[589.27,"But just to repeat, cherub is not a little baby with wings."],[593.21,"It's not a little fat baby selling you toilet paper or something."],[596.09,"Angel Soft. Um those are Cupids."],[601.42,"From Roman paganism."],[603.42,"Um but uh a a cherub, a kerub was, if you've seen the uh a Babylonian, what's called what they called a lamasu."],[613.9,"It's uh looks kind of like a Sphinx."],[617.2,"It's got like the lion body and the big wings, and then the human head with the ringlet beard and the you know that is what a cherub is."],[625.6,"Okay And uh the uh so that's a cherub."],[635.0,"The cherubim is just plural."],[636.84,"Eam is the Hebrew plural ending."],[639.5,"It literally means a living creature."],[641.34,"That's why they get called living creatures in the English translations of Daniel and uh Ezekiel and uh Revelation."],[650.3,"Uh so that's what what is when we get to the Ark of the Covenant, that's what's on the Ark of the Covenant."],[656.27,"When we say that Christ in the liturgy is seated upon the Keruvim, that's what we mean, not a whole bunch of little babies."],[664.17,"Um, but what what a a uh Keruv, cherub did, in the Babylonian version, is they were a throne guardian for the divine throne of one of the gods."],[686.11,"And uh that's the place where we see them in in both the old and new testaments."],[694.51,"The New Testament we're talking mainly about revelation."],[697.17,"But they're surrounding the throne."],[698.95,"God is enthroned upon them."],[702.07,"If your question is, why does God need these weird angelic beings."],[709.13,"With multiple heads and animal bodies to protect his throne."],[712.57,"He's they're not protecting his throne from us."],[715.69,"They're protecting us from his throne."],[719.9,"They're keeping people away from the divine presence, which would consume them."],[727.92,"You get death by holiness if you get too close and you're not, and you're not wholly impure."],[734.16,"So that's the Babylonian version."],[737.36,"The Egyptian version of the same creature, the throne guardian creature, were uh seraphim, literally."],[751.2,"So the word the word seraph is a word that Hebrew borrowed from Egyptian, is a the Egyptian word for snake or serpent."],[765.56,"And they borrowed it just the plural put a Hebrew ending on it."],[770.57,"And uh seraph in Hebrew uh now that they've borrowed it can have the same a similar kind of range of meanings."],[783.13,"One of those is the burning one, it was a participle, um, and they're generally presented as as winged serpents."],[796.94,"So the burning being conne concnetedc.ted to serpents was likely to their venom"],[803.94,"And in when we see them in Egypt, the winged part is the cobra hood."],[812.82,"Now, in later Israelite stuff, like we have coins from the northern kingdom of Israel that have seraphim on them, and they're literally like a snake with wings."],[826.12,"Like on its back."],[827.32,"So that gets literalized later on in Old Testament history."],[831.64,"But the Egyptian version is sort of a cobra with a hood, and the burning part is the bite, the venom."],[838.21,"So this is why, when you look at, say, King Tutankhamun's sarcophagus."],[843.25,"This is probably the most for us in the U.S."],[848.49,"Image of that, he has a cobra on his forehead."],[853.79,"And this is why the Pharaoh's headdress looks like a cobra's hood."],[861.33,"This is the idea of that divine being."],[863.01,"Of course, they considered Pharaoh to be a god."],[866.14,"And so it was their protecting."],[869.72,"The uh the throne of the god."],[872.84,"So this is what, and it's interesting throughout the throughout the Torah, the Petateuch of of one these books, there's this mix of Mesopotamian and Egyptian imagery, which is sort of exactly what you would expect based on our introduction."],[894.26,"If you have this first layer from the Israelites coming out of Egypt, that then gets kind of translated into a later version of Hebrew, with some edits and some updates."],[908.06,"That's where the Mesopotamian influence comes in, and you get both of these together."],[914.82,"So that's the kind of creature we're talking about here."],[918.18,"This is this is an angelic being"],[921.54,"This is a shining being."],[924.23,"Who is at this point in God's presence, which is what paradise is, as we talked about, what Eden is."],[930.75,"This is the place where God dwells, garden on a mountain."],[935.36,"And so he's one of the other creatures there."],[939.68,"Yes, sir. So how related is this to this the word that's used about the snakes that Moses has to put the bronze."],[948.72,"Same word. It's the same word."],[950.24,"So is it are we supposed to have the same kind of idea that of those being like shiny one?"],[958.56,"Well, no, that's not the word that's used for the snakes on the ground."],[961.52,"It's the one that's used for the bronze one."],[964.24,"Oh, okay. So this one isn't part of the uh animals who were named."],[971.84,"Right And so um, I know we talked about this back in Revelation 12, but it bears repeating."],[983.23,"Because everybody has a huge dose of John Milton in their heads."],[989.76,"That um that at some point unrecorded in the Bible before the world was created, before humans were created."],[999.14,"That that Satan and the devil, who are the same person, uh, or the same angel, led this rebellion, and the angels fought each other, and and uh uh Satan lost, and he'd rather rule in hell than serve in heaven like Milton's romantic figure and and went down to hell."],[1022.92,"So he was he was already like in hell at the time this is happening."],[1028.1,"And biggest problem is as I mentioned, that's nowhere in the Bible."],[1032.82,"Um it's also nowhere in the church fathers."],[1037.86,"It's really nowhere until you get to close to Milton."],[1042.1,"Um so um as we talked about when we were talking about Genesis 1, uh church fathers put the creation of the angelic beings on different days, usually the first day or the fourth day."],[1057.22,"And we talked about why those are connected and why those two"],[1064.86,"And and we're well, I'll go back to we talked about him a bunch in uh when we were working our way through Revelation."],[1074.19,"That's St. Andrew of Caesarea, uh, who wrote uh the commentary on the book of Revelation in the sixth century that finally helped get Revelation accepted as canon in in the Eastern churches."],[1092.67,"Um in that commentary, when he's talking about Revelation 12, he says, as I am wont to quote, he says, we must accept what all the fathers say, which is that after the creation of the world, the devil fell through envy."],[1112.55,"We'll talk about that fell through envy part here as we go forward."],[1116.63,"But for now, the after the creation of the world part."],[1120.87,"And so St."],[1121.83,"Andrew in the 6th century is looking at all of the Christian interpreters that he has access to, which was a lot, because Caesarea was a major Christian city."],[1134.52,"And going through these libraries of all the Christian commentary up to the 6th century, and he says, This is what everybody says."],[1141.64,"Not just this is my preferred opinion."],[1144.12,"It's this is what Christianity teaches thus far."],[1149.94,"So, yes, at this point, Paul hasn't happy yet."],[1153.14,"We're about to read about it."],[1155.48,"Coming up. And what it means."],[1172.89,"Because Milton's idea is that uh the devil was going to somehow depose God and make himself God."],[1186.25,"My question being, how?"],[1195.57,"Like. He's a creature?"],[1198.05,"Like, if you wanted to do that, what would be step one?"],[1203.73,"Uh Because, like, this isn't like Bruce Almighty or something."],[1208.1,"God is not just like a being with superpowers."],[1211.22,"He's God. Like, there's not a way to make him not God and make yourself God."],[1220.68,"this This would be like one of my dogs deciding to make me a dog and himself a human."],[1229.54,"Like, how are you gonna do that?"],[1229.58,"Like. Like Good luck."],[1238.36,"Um. So yeah, it's just not like a feasible even thing."],[1245.9,"But we'll we'll we'll talk more about as we go forward about what's going on."],[1249.58,"But so that's who this is."],[1252.7,"Who's now talking uh to the woman?"],[1255.66,"Can I ask you?"],[1256.46,"Yes, sir. Um the mention of wild animals sounds like they're to us at least that they're including the serpent among the wild animals, but um at least that's something different from that."],[1269.83,"It doesn't say he's more cutting than the other."],[1272.47,"the other From creatures."],[1274.73,"So it doesn't have a distinction from the wild animals."],[1275.33,"Yeah He's not like the animals who at we just saw Adam naming."],[1285.17,"He is a different kind of thing."],[1288.59,"Like a bear would not have been able to talk even to eating the apple."],[1296.75,"Because he can't talk for one thing."],[1298.96,"But he's a different kind of."],[1301.02,"So he's not in that category of the being the the creatures that were paraded before Adam in the last chapter."],[1311.58,"Okay, so and then so now he comes and he speaks to the woman and says, has God indeed said you shall not eat from every tree of the garden so notice already there's something a little odd there in how he phrases that remember the the commandment was you may eat from every tree of the garden except this one and so he c his response is has he not has he uh has he said to you you shall not eat from every tree of the garden?"],[1355.6,"Well the answer to that is no."],[1358.1,"He didn't say you shall not eat of every like it's repositioning."],[1365.72,"Also note, this is the first theological conversation."],[1370.6,"This is why you should not argue about theology on the internet."],[1374.12,"This is the beginning of that tradition."],[1379.84,"What I mean by that is, what I mean by that is, this is the first time someone has had a conversation about God as an object."],[1395.84,"Not God speaking to people or God speaking to a person and a person speaking back to God."],[1401.58,"But two creatures having a conversation about God."],[1404.94,"As if he's not there"],[1413.47,"So this is even the way he begins this is problematic."],[1420.57,"There's a there's there's already something going on here, some gamesmanship at work."],[1429.93,"So verse 2."],[1432.65,"And the woman said to the serpent, We may eat the fruit from the trees of the garden, but from the fruit of the tree in the middle of the garden, God said, You shall not eat from it, nor shall you touch it lest you die."],[1447.37,"So she doesn't immediately fall for the trick question."],[1453.55,"She says, This is what we were told."],[1457.51,"And actually, it wasn't her."],[1459.27,"You remember that command was given to Adam before she existed."],[1462.84,"But you notice there's been an added bit, and we don't know whether Adam added this bit or she added this bit."],[1469.9,"Maybe Adam when he told her, but the don't even touch it part."],[1474.88,"Don't even go near it."],[1475.84,"You know what?"],[1476.16,"Don't even go near it."],[1477.04,"Don't mess with that tree."],[1480.07,"But this is this is something that becomes a major part of Jewish interpretation of the commandments."],[1489.43,"Already by the time you get to the New Testament"],[1494.97,"This idea of building a fence around the commandments."],[1498.17,"We've talked about this before back uh when we were talking about the Pharisees."],[1502.81,"Um, because the idea was, you know what?"],[1506.55,"We don't want to sin."],[1507.67,"We don't want to break the commandments."],[1509.83,"So we build a fence a few feet away from actually breaking it."],[1513.91,"And that way we don't even get close to breaking it."],[1516.65,"So can't take the name of the Lord in vain if you never ever say Yahweh."],[1522.51,"If you just never say the name, then you can't take it in vain."],[1526.47,"Um, I'm supposed to work on the Sabbath."],[1530.07,"What does that mean?"],[1530.95,"Well, you can only walk this far, you could only do that, you could only do all these things to stay away from breaking the commandments."],[1541.68,"So again, we have a theological discussion going on here."],[1546.74,"It's like we don't go anywhere near it."],[1549.08,"We don't have anything to do with that tree."],[1552.09,"But he says uh you will die, and it's occurs to me that there hasn't been any death at all yet."],[1558.89,"So with nothing that's died."],[1561.77,"What? Would she understand what he meant"],[1565.93,"Uh no, and we'll get to that later on in chapter three."],[1569.53,"Okay. Um. Is there something directly addressed at that?"],[1573.41,"Because yes, this is a you will die as a hypothetical thing at this point"],[1581.3,"Um we'll get into exactly what that means."],[1586.98,"But so she accurately, a little over accurately, building a little answer, describes this is what this is what God said."],[1597.98,"Verse 4. Then the serpent said to the woman, you shall not die by death."],[1605.18,"Wherever that's the we we talked about um that when God first gave the commandment, it translated it the same way, that die by death thing."],[1616.7,"Oh yeah That was reminds me of this old movie, Murder by Death."],[1623.46,"Have you seen?"],[1624.34,"No, I've seen it."],[1625.06,"Oh, okay. There's sort of a parody of Agatha Christie mysteries."],[1630.34,"But uh that's a way of there's there's a a Hebrew construction called an infinitive absolute where you have a verb and then you put the infinitive of the verb right after it."],[1643.78,"So if you translated that really literally, it'd be like dying, you will die."],[1649.16,"And that's a way of being sort of super emphatic."],[1652.05,"Like the King James, I think, does that as you will surely die."],[1656.31,"And this is die by death, I just find supposed to dying by what?"],[1660.55,"That's what other thing can you die by"],[1665.85,"But that's the idea there."],[1667.55,"Like, this will certainly happen."],[1670.0,"So, verse 4 here, in his rejoinder, the serpent now directly contradicts what God told them."],[1681.44,"So he said, You will."],[1682.72,"Serpent says, you will not."],[1687.36,"Verse 5, for God knows in the day you eat from it, your eyes will be opened."],[1694.64,"And you will be like gods, knowing good and evil."],[1701.44,"Okay. So again, this is some of that subtleness."],[1709.28,"Because uh, in a certain way, that second part of the statement is true."],[1717.53,"If you understand what gods here is referring to."],[1722.62,"What is gods here referring to?"],[1725.26,"So this is we're Genesis, so we might as well have this discussion now."],[1732.06,"Um gods is used in the plural, all over the place in the Old Testament."],[1744.94,"It's actually more common in the Greek. than in the Hebrew"],[1750.66,"And the later you go in Jewish history, the more common it becomes."],[1760.9,"To use gods in the plural."],[1762.74,"Why is that important?"],[1764.18,"That it becomes more common."],[1766.34,"Because uh a lot of our friends, led by our 18th and 19th century German friends, uh want to create this sort of evolutionary thing where the Israelites started out as polytheists and then at some point later became monotheists."],[1786.02,"So they started out believing in and worshipping a whole bunch of gods, and then later on they only believed that one God existed."],[1796.7,"Neither of those are true."],[1801.82,"So the idea of monotheism meaning there's only one God that exists is an idea that begins in the late 16th and early 17th century."],[1818.7,"You literally don't find the term before that."],[1829.02,"Because monos and theos are both Greek words."],[1834.48,"But the church fathers, writing in Greek, never talk about monotheism."],[1836.24,"Never use the word monotheism"],[1842.92,"And they do use the word polytheism."],[1845.24,"They call the pagans polytheists."],[1847.32,"You believe in many gods, but they don't call themselves monotheists."],[1852.68,"The word they use for themselves is monarchia, meaning monarchy, one first principle."],[1861.53,"One God who is unlike any other being which is called God."],[1866.86,"And they're getting that from Saint Paul, that direct language."],[1870.54,"Remember, Saint Paul said there are many things that are called gods, and indeed there are many gods and many lords, but for us there's one God the Father and one Lord Jesus Christ."],[1882.52,"So the term God, lowercase G in the plural, it's all over the place."],[1890.6,"It's all over the place of the Dead Sea Scrolls."],[1893.34,"The Melchizedek scroll of the Dead Sea Scrolls talks about the in the end times, Melchizedek, the Messiah, will come with all the righteous gods."],[1904.96,"And you're going, wait, I thought these people were Jewish."],[1908.88,"Yeah, these are like second century BC Jewish people, not like some early polytheist Jewish people."],[1915.09,"Because they're using that word in a different way."],[1919.5,"Than the way we use it."],[1922.88,"So that word got used, that plural word got used just to refer to, like sometimes it gets translated divine beings."],[1931.28,"Usually it gets covered up in English translations."],[1933.84,"They don't like using it because people freak out when they see gods."],[1938.45,"But um that's used just to refer to uh spiritual beings, divine beings, what we would call angels, demons, etc."],[1950.15,"Just get called gods, and they're totally fine with that."],[1954.68,"And they have various ways of indicating that God."],[1958.13,"Yahweh, the God of Israel, is different than those other spiritual beings."],[1968.91,"Number one being he created them."],[1971.89,"So he's eternal, they're not."],[1974.15,"All of these other differences."],[1975.75,"One of the main linguistic ways, like in Greek, is they put the article before God."],[1982.63,"So you would literally translate it as the God."],[1987.68,"That's a handy way, like the way we use the capital G as opposed to the lowercase G, they use the article as opposed to without the article."],[1997.05,"Um, this is super common and nothing to freak out about."],[2003.89,"So, what what the serpent is saying here is not if you eat the apple, you will turn into God."],[2014.43,"He's saying if you eat the apple, you will be like these divine beings, like the serpent."],[2022.51,"You'll be like one of us instead of one of you."],[2027.92,"And God doesn't want that to happen."],[2031.36,"That's why he told you not to do it."],[2037.41,"So that's the lie he's telling."],[2042.49,"Which is a half truth."],[2046.17,"Because what we're gonna see is that when they they do eat from it, do in a certain way become like those beings."],[2055.73,"Because the serpent already knows good and evil."],[2059.97,"He's more about the evil right now than the good."],[2065.73,"But he knows good and evil."],[2067.73,"And Adam and Eve don't."],[2069.01,"As we talked about, that's a mark of their innocence."],[2071.35,"This is not talking about them being perfect, it's talking about them being innocent, like children."],[2075.59,"Because knowing good and evil, we just heard from Isaiah in the Christmas services."],[2082.25,"In the prophecy that a virgin would bear a child."],[2088.03,"It said, before he is old enough to know the no good and evil, this will happen."],[2091.31,"That means coming to maturity, coming to right"],[2097.17,"And so what the devil is offering here is God created you."],[2110.29,"Here's the shortcut."],[2115.43,"That's why he doesn't want you to eat from it."],[2117.67,"It's not that it's dangerous for you to eat from it before you're ready, before you are mature."],[2123.51,"It's that he doesn't want you to do this adult thing as a kid."],[2130.97,"He doesn't want you."],[2132.23,"And we're gonna see this is this is gonna be a pattern, especially here in the first 11 chapters of Genesis, but even going forward, of how the devil, demonic beings, Satan, how they work, is offering kinds of knowledge, kinds of things to humanity before they're ready for it, knowing that humans will use it in destructive ways."],[2163.64,"Knowing that it'll be destructive."],[2171.72,"Verse 6. So when the woman saw the tree was good for food, was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree beautiful to contemplate."],[2177.0,"to Sheok its fruit and ate."],[2180.4,"I like that translation too."],[2184.64,"She also gave it to her husband with her, and he ate."],[2189.2,"So a couple things here."],[2192.32,"First, uh, upon hearing this, she doesn't say anything to God."],[2202.96,"Again, this whole conversation is happening as if God's off somewhere."],[2208.0,"That doesn't hear or see what's going on."],[2210.64,"She doesn't ask God for clarification."],[2212.88,"She doesn'.t She just kind of looks at the tree."],[2216.96,"The tree looks good."],[2219.52,"The fruit looks good."],[2221.44,"Seems right in her own eyes."],[2222.0,"and So she goes ahead does it."],[2227.45,"And then gives it to Adam, who's with her."],[2234.65,"What does this mean?"],[2236.09,"Adam's been standing there the whole time."],[2242.09,"He's been standing there the whole time."],[2244.89,"This whole conversation."],[2247.63,"This isn't like Serpent shows up while she's all by herself and tricks her."],[2253.63,"He's standing right there."],[2256.75,"Letting this all unfold."],[2260.29,"Letting this all unfold."],[2261.65,"She didn't know it's not going to be."],[2264.05,"Yeah, let's watch her eat it and see what happens."],[2265.17,"Yeah So, what's the clear problem here?"],[2276.65,"He should have shut this down, at some point."],[2280.02,"He's already complicit."],[2282.52,"Yes. Part of why he's there is to tend the garden."],[2287.9,"To protect this sacred space in which he's been put."],[2291.9,"As we talked about, this follows the creation of the world followed this pattern of the creation of a temple, in the ancient Near East."],[2302.08,"And Adam's there as this priest."],[2304.26,"Well, what are you supposed to do?"],[2305.38,"You're supposed to protect it."],[2306.51,"You should have driven the serpent out of there."],[2309.77,"Or at least it's something to God, like, hey, what's what's up with this guy?"],[2316.95,"But not only does he not do that."],[2320.28,"At least when his wife doesn't instantly drop dead, he decides to disobey too."],[2330.48,"Disobey too. And just follows her lead."],[2333.84,"Like, oh, okay."],[2341.84,"Then the eyes of the two were opened, and they knew they were naked."],[2347.12,"ed. So they sew fig leaves together and made themselves coverings"],[2354.44,"So remember, when they were created, we talked about how they were naked."],[2359.08,"They were not ashamed, it said."],[2360.52,"And we talked about how that's more of that innocence imagery of children."],[2365.66,"It's okay for little kids up to a certain age to run around naked."],[2370.24,"Then they reach a certain age and that stops."],[2373.41,"And that's appropriate."],[2374.95,"And so the idea that they were just sort of walking around naked and unashamed, meant that they were still in this state of innocence."],[2383.01,"Well, they eat from the tree, innocence gone"],[2387.41,"nocence gone. And once in is, then shame comes"],[2396.22,"And they go and they they cover themselves."],[2399.28,"Have you ever noticed in in kids' books of the story of Adam and Eve."],[2402.74,"Like before this, like they're always standing behind these bushes that have branches, like in convenient places."],[2408.58,"But anyway, so now we don't need those anymore because we have the the uh sewn together fig leaves."],[2415.3,"But so they have to they have to cover themselves."],[2417.3,"So that's the immediate effect."],[2421.26,"Is this loss of innocence, this loss of purity."],[2426.3,"They've leapfrogged, but in the wrong way."],[2429.44,"They already knew good, because remember, as God was creating. everything he finished, he said he called it good"],[2438.76,"Everything except Adam until after his wife was made."],[2444.12,"Remember, he said it's not good that man should be alone on the earth."],[2447.69,"But then it was good afterwards."],[2449.31,"That situation was dealt with."],[2453.37,"So now, so what they've gotten from eating of the tree is the knowledge of evil."],[2458.89,"That's what they've gained."],[2460.57,"The knowledge of something other than the good."],[2464.13,"And knowledge here is not just like data."],[2473.42,"Like, oh, you mean it's it's possible to disobey God?"],[2477.07,"Because they just did, clearly."],[2481.07,"There was that concept."],[2484.57,"It's not just raw data."],[2486.17,"The word Yada to know in in Hebrew is the word that's going to be used at the end of this chapter to say Adam knew his wife and she brought forth a son."],[2500.35,"So this is not."],[2505.23,"I remember way back when we were talking about Romans."],[2508.35,"We were working through Romans."],[2509.71,"I talked about the difference between knowledge and knowledge about."],[2517.12,"So there is information that like old friends of my wife know about my wife that I don't know."],[2529.96,"Her doctor has data right in the chart in the file about my wife that I don't know."],[2540.2,"But none of those folks know my wife the way I know my wife."],[2545.08,"So there's a difference between knowing someone or something and knowing about it."],[2552.76,"So eating of the tree, and that's why it's eating from a tree."],[2559.0,"Is not knowledge about."],[2560.68,"It's not that they gained some theoretical knowledge about what evil might be."],[2563.16,"In their brain"],[2567.17,"It's they came to know evil."],[2572.13,"The ancient understanding of eating is you eat something, it becomes part of you."],[2579.17,"It becomes part of your body."],[2582.37,"So they have taken evil into themselves now."],[2590.45,"So it's not a loss of innocence in the sense of they lost their naivete."],[2597.27,"It's loss of innocence, as in they now have."],[2599.91,"they now have. They didn't lose the principle of good within them, but also this principle of evil within them"],[2610.19,"That's become a part of them also."],[2617.39,"Verse 8. Then they heard the voice of the Lord God walking in the garden that afternoon."],[2626.59,"And Adam and his wife hid themselves within the tree in the middle of the garden from the presence of the Lord God."],[2635.95,"And that's a pretty literal translation."],[2639.79,"Yahweh, who is God, capital G God, is walking in the garden."],[2647.16,"Um that's how it's described."],[2651.04,"And in the Hebrew it says that he comes walking in the the heat of the day."],[2656.52,"The cool of the day here."],[2657.96,"Yeah, I know, which is bizarre."],[2659.8,"Well, what it literally says, okay."],[2662.6,"It's NIV. What are you gonna do?"],[2664.68,"Uh anyway. What it literally says in Hebrew is uh Ruachayom, which means the spirit of the day."],[2675.22,"So the heat of the day is treating the word spirit there as referring to like a hot wind."],[2684.5,"Because the word spirit can also mean wind."],[2687.56,"Um, and that's where they're getting heat of the day."],[2690.44,"I guess the NIV translators thought that meant breeze."],[2693.56,"I don't know."],[2694.28,"I don't know what you get cool of the day."],[2695.16,"But um But the fact that it's the word spirit is also kind of important."],[2708.04,"In the sense of the spirit and the presence of God."],[2715.29,"And the fact that it's Hayom is also interesting."],[2722.57,"That it's the day."],[2726.97,"We've talked about later on, in the Hebrew Bible and the Old Testament, when the day is referred to, it's the day of Yahweh, the day of the Lord."],[2737.51,"the day. That's what means"],[2742.97,"And that usage gets carried over into the New Testament."],[2746.57,"Where there are passages that refer to the day or that day, and it's referring to the day of the Lord."],[2755.34,"What is the day of the Lord?"],[2759.02,"It's the day of judgment."],[2761.03,"It's the day of visitation."],[2762.39,"It's another thing it's called in the Old Testament."],[2765.27,"That's the day where Yahweh comes and things get sorted out."],[2770.23,"We've talked about how judgment is things being put back in order."],[2775.0,"Yahweh visiting his people is not grandpa visiting for tea and scones."],[2779.64,"Some stuff's gonna get sorted out"],[2779.74,"It's wait till your dad gets home."],[2785.08,"Um when it says that he comes in the spirit of the day after what's just happened."],[2796.73,"That's the resonance."],[2798.18,"He's he's coming, he's now going to have to."],[2802.16,"Injustice has just happened, things are out of whack, things aren't good anymore in his creation, and so now some things are gonna have to be sorted out."],[2813.14,"But also, notice he comes walking and they hear it."],[2818.5,"So mark something."],[2821.62,"This is the first of many places we're gonna see in the Torah, in the Pentateuch, where God has a body."],[2834.06,"And he does things like walking and eating."],[2838.38,"And it says they heard the voice symbol."],[2840.38,"Is that like the word?"],[2841.66,"Yeah, that's a weird."],[2843.82,"Yeah, I think that's where they're getting that."],[2846.22,"I think the Greek translation pulled that from the Aramaic."],[2850.38,"Let me explain that."],[2852.22,"So there are all these places."],[2856.14,"There's this tension in the Torah, in the Pedateuch."],[2860.14,"Where on one hand it repeatedly gets said, no one can see God and live."],[2865.02,"And on the other hand, there are all these people who see. God and live"],[2870.66,"So that's a certain kind of tension."],[2873.84,"In Exodus chapter 33, in the same chapter, I forget how many verses, it's nine or ten verses apart, I think."],[2883.12,"We're told that Moses spoke to God frequently face to face on Mount Sinai."],[2888.48,"And then we're told he asks to see God's glory, and God says, You can't see me and live."],[2898.16,"So, tension. And one of the ways that got worked out is that in the Aramaic, what's usually referred to as the Targums."],[2910.08,"Not all Targums are Aramai Ac.ramaic, but the Targums are the most common ones"],[2915.56,"Their translations, but they're loose trained."],[2920.28,"They're like the message of the Hebrew Bible."],[2924.84,"Or the amplified Hebrew Bible."],[2928.04,"There's some more."],[2931.37,"But what they did to try to handle that issue is they took the Aramaic word memrah, which means word."],[2944.09,"And wherever God was walking, eating, had hands."],[2952.06,"Any of these things, they added the word memorization."],[2960.02,"So instead of just saying Moses spoke to Yahweh face to face."],[2967.7,"It was Moses spoke to Memrah Yahweh face to face."],[2971.94,"The word of Yahweh face to face."],[2976.63,"And that idea, is part of the general view in what's called Second Temple Judaism, right before the New Testament, that there is this intermediary figure between Yahweh who cannot be seen and creatures, who is often just identified as Yahweh, just identified as God."],[3006.74,"But can be seen."],[3010.15,"And that happens in different ways, but so the word of Yahweh, the Memra Yahweh in Aramaic, the Dabar Yahweh in Hebrew, becomes one of the names for that figure."],[3028.71,"And spoilers, Christians very quickly ball all of those figures together and say that's Jesus."],[3036.64,"And when I say very quickly, St."],[3038.96,"Justin Martyr does that."],[3040.52,"Like in the middle of the second century."],[3042.08,"He has just this list"],[3046.6,"He says that Christ appeared in the Old Testament as the angel of the Lord, the word of the Lord."],[3052.7,"That just lists all of them."],[3054.78,"And it's like that's all him."],[3056.54,"Is this behind the prologue of John's Gospel?"],[3059.02,"Yes. The memorable part is behind the prologue of St."],[3062.78,"John's Gospel. When he talks about in the beginning was the word, and the word was with God."],[3067.34,"This is like a Jewish thing."],[3069.96,"His ritual audience would have known what he was talking about."],[3073.77,"The word became flesh."],[3075.15,"They would have been like, okay, you are identifying Jesus of Nazareth as this figure."],[3083.11,"And so from, a Christian perspective, again, starting in the second century, it is completely appropriate to read this as saying Christ came walking in the garden."],[3095.47,"Or God the Son, or the second person of the tree."],[3099.63,"However, you want to identify him at this point, that's who this is."],[3109.87,"And that's not disconnected from that element of judgment and the day."],[3115.23,"Because what does Christ say in St."],[3116.67,"John's Gospel? Even the Father judges no one."],[3122.59,"All judgment has been given to the Son."],[3126.69,"He doesn't say from now on."],[3128.37,"So, from the beginning"],[3134.38,"And so they they go, when they hear him coming, rather than greeting him, they go and hide."],[3143.71,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[3151.07,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3156.43,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.08,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where we are","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up in <b>Genesis 3</b> - \"nothing really eventful is gonna happen here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not re-doing the whole <b>intro to Torah / Genesis</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gen 1:1-2:1</b>: creation of the world, structured around <b>seven days</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gen 2</b>: creation of humanity - <b>man first, then woman</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"quickest review ever\" - more time for chapter 3</li><li class=\"d0\">Opens for leftover questions or comments - none; on to <b>3:1</b></li></ul>"},{"t":106.82,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 3:1 - The serpent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:1</b>: serpent \"more cunning than all the wild animals the Lord God made\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Bunch to cover in one verse - first, <b>the serpent</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not</b> Kipling's <i>Just So</i> story of how the snake lost his legs</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"snakes used to have legs like lizards\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not</b> a talking-snake story either</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"animals used to talk in paradise\" and lost the ability</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew word = <i>nakash</i></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew words have <b>triliteral roots</b> - three consonants</li><li class=\"d1\">added letters often show part of speech - but not always</li><li class=\"d1\">no vowels in the original, so <b>room for interpretation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">esp. in poetry: <b>wordplay</b> - leave a word ambiguous to mean both</li></ul>"},{"t":305.98,"lv":3,"h":"Nakash: three meanings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Nakash</i> has no consonantal markers of part of speech</li><li class=\"d0\">As <b>noun</b> → <b>serpent</b> (why translators go that way)</li><li class=\"d0\">As <b>adjective</b> → <b>clever / wise one</b></li><li class=\"d0\">As <b>participle</b> (verb used as noun) → <b>brazen one / shining one</b></li><li class=\"d1\">brazen = bronze</li><li class=\"d0\">All three meanings included</li><li class=\"d0\">\"more cunning\" (Orthodox Study Bible) bolsters one: <b>cunning, devious, slippery</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one English translation: <b>\"subtle\"</b> - older sense; not hinting, but this idea</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: not just a talking snake - a <b>serpentine being</b>: <b>shining, glowing, brazen</b>, and <b>wise / clever / devious</b></li></ul>"},{"t":456.26,"lv":3,"h":"Who is this? Ezekiel and Isaiah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">His <b>introduction</b> - just shows up; won't appear in this form again after Gen 3</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John</b> refers to it, but not as a character under this name</li><li class=\"d0\">Two places on the figure who becomes <b>the devil</b>: <b>Isaiah</b> and <b>Ezekiel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Prophecy against <b>king of Tyre</b>, in two pieces</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>prince of Tyre</b> = the human king - you can tell</li><li class=\"d1\">then switches to <b>king of Tyre</b> = spiritual power behind him, <b>Baal</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ezekiel</b>: similar passage - divine figure <b>cast down out of heaven</b> (casting down later this chapter)</li><li class=\"d1\">walks on the <b>mountain of Assembly</b>, <b>mountain of Eden</b> - Eden as a mountain</li><li class=\"d1\">there called a <b>guardian cherub</b> - <i>keruv</i></li></ul>"},{"t":589.27,"lv":2,"h":"What a cherub is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Cherub is <b>not a little baby with wings</b> - \"not a little fat baby selling you toilet paper\" (Angel Soft)</li><li class=\"d1\">those are <b>Cupids</b>, from Roman paganism</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Kerub</i> = Babylonian <b>lamasu</b>: <b>lion body, big wings, human head</b> with ringlet beard - like a Sphinx</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>-im</i> = Hebrew plural; <i>cherubim</i> literally <b>living creatures</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why \"living creatures\" in <b>Daniel, Ezekiel, Revelation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">On the <b>Ark of the Covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">liturgy: Christ \"seated upon the <i>Keruvim</i>\" = these, \"not a whole bunch of little babies\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Babylonian role: <b>throne guardian</b> for a god's throne; same in OT and NT (esp. Revelation)</li><li class=\"d1\">God enthroned upon them</li><li class=\"d1\">not protecting his throne <b>from us</b> - protecting <b>us from his throne</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keep people from the divine presence - \"<b>death by holiness</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":737.36,"lv":2,"h":"Seraphim: the Egyptian version","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Egyptian throne-guardian = <b>seraphim</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Seraph</i> borrowed from Egyptian word for <b>snake / serpent</b>, Hebrew plural ending added</li><li class=\"d1\">range of meanings: <b>the burning one</b> (participle)</li><li class=\"d1\">winged serpents - burning = <b>venom</b>; the wings = <b>cobra hood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Later Israelite: <b>coins from northern kingdom</b> show seraphim as literal snake with wings - \"literalized\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Egyptian: <b>Tutankhamun's</b> sarcophagus - cobra on forehead; Pharaoh's headdress = cobra's hood</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharaoh considered a god; cobra protects the god's throne</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah mixes <b>Mesopotamian and Egyptian</b> imagery - as expected from intro</li><li class=\"d1\">first layer from Israelites leaving <b>Egypt</b>; later edits/updates bring <b>Mesopotamian</b> influence</li></ul>"},{"t":914.82,"lv":3,"h":"A shining being in God's presence; Q: Moses' bronze snake","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">So: an <b>angelic, shining being</b></li><li class=\"d0\">At this point in <b>God's presence</b> = what paradise / Eden is</li><li class=\"d1\">place where God dwells - <b>garden on a mountain</b></li><li class=\"d0\">One of the other creatures there</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: how related to the word for the snakes Moses puts on the <b>bronze</b> pole?</li><li class=\"d1\">same word as the <b>bronze</b> one - not the word for snakes on the ground</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: so not one of the <b>animals named</b>? - Right</li></ul>"},{"t":971.84,"lv":2,"h":"The Milton myth: when did the devil fall?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Everyone has \"a huge dose of <b>John Milton</b>\" in their heads</li><li class=\"d1\">before creation, <b>Satan</b> led a rebellion, angels fought, he lost</li><li class=\"d1\">\"better to rule in hell than serve in heaven\" - already in hell at this point</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: <b>nowhere in the Bible</b>; <b>nowhere in the church fathers</b> - not till close to Milton</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers put creation of angels on <b>day 1 or day 4</b> (see Gen 1)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Andrew of Caesarea</b>, 6th c., commentary on <b>Revelation</b> (helped get it accepted in the East)</li><li class=\"d1\">on <b>Rev 12</b>: \"we must accept what all the fathers say\" - devil fell <b>after the creation of the world</b>, <b>through envy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"fell through envy\" - to come</li><li class=\"d0\">He surveyed all Christian commentary to the 6th c. (Caesarea a major city, big libraries)</li><li class=\"d1\">not his preferred opinion - <b>what Christianity teaches</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1172.89,"lv":3,"h":"Milton's idea: how depose God?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Milton: devil to <b>depose God</b> and make himself God</li><li class=\"d0\">\"How?\" - he's a <b>creature</b>; what would step one be?</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <i>Bruce Almighty</i> - God isn't a being with superpowers; he's <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no way to make him not God and yourself God</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like one of my dogs deciding to make me a dog and himself a human\" - \"good luck\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not even a feasible idea; more on what's actually going on as we go</li></ul>"},{"t":1249.58,"lv":3,"h":"Q: is the serpent among the wild animals?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">So that's who is now <b>talking to the woman</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"wild animals\" seems to include the serpent</li><li class=\"d1\">text doesn't say he's more cunning <b>than the others</b> as a distinction</li><li class=\"d1\">but he's <b>a different kind of thing</b> - not like the animals Adam named</li><li class=\"d1\">a bear couldn't talk, even eating the apple</li><li class=\"d1\">not in the category of creatures paraded before Adam in ch. 2</li></ul>"},{"t":1311.58,"lv":2,"h":"3:1 - \"Has God indeed said...?\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Serpent to woman: \"has God indeed said, you shall not eat from <b>every</b> tree?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Already odd: the command was <b>\"you may eat from every tree except this one\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">answer is no - he didn't say that; <b>repositioning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First <b>theological conversation</b> - \"why you should not argue about theology on the internet\"</li><li class=\"d1\">first time someone talks about <b>God as an object</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not God speaking to a person and a person answering</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two creatures discussing God</b> - \"as if he's not there\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Even the opening is problematic - <b>gamesmanship</b> at work</li></ul>"},{"t":1429.93,"lv":2,"h":"3:2-3 - The woman's answer and the fence","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:2-3</b>: \"may eat the fruit of the trees... but of the tree in the middle... <b>nor shall you touch it lest you die</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">She doesn't fall for the trick question - \"this is what we were told\"</li><li class=\"d1\">actually command was given to <b>Adam before she existed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Added bit: <b>\"nor touch it\"</b> - Adam added it? she? Maybe Adam when he told her</li><li class=\"d0\">Becomes major part of Jewish interpretation: <b>building a fence around the commandments</b> (cf. <b>Pharisees</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">stay a few feet from breaking it; never say <b>Yahweh</b> → can't take name in vain</li><li class=\"d1\">Sabbath: only walk this far, do only these things</li><li class=\"d0\">Again a theological discussion: \"don't go anywhere near that tree\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1552.09,"lv":3,"h":"Q: would she understand \"die\"?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"you will die\" - no death yet; would she understand?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"no\" - get to it later in ch. 3</li><li class=\"d1\">at this point a <b>hypothetical</b>; what it means later</li><li class=\"d0\">She accurately - \"a little over accurately\" - describes what God said</li></ul>"},{"t":1597.98,"lv":2,"h":"3:4 - \"You shall not die by death\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:4</b>: serpent: \"you shall not die by death\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same translation as God's original commandment</li><li class=\"d1\">aside: old movie <i>Murder by Death</i> - parody of Agatha Christie</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <b>infinitive absolute</b>: verb + its infinitive right after</li><li class=\"d1\">literally \"dying, you will die\" = <b>super emphatic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">King James: \"you will <b>surely</b> die\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"die by what? what other thing can you die by?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>this will certainly happen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Serpent now <b>directly contradicts</b> God: God said \"you will\" - serpent says \"<b>you will not</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1687.36,"lv":2,"h":"3:5 - \"You will be like gods\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:5</b>: \"God knows in the day you eat... eyes opened... <b>like gods, knowing good and evil</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Subtleness\" - second part is <b>true</b>, if you understand what \"gods\" means</li><li class=\"d0\">Good place to have the discussion: <b>\"gods\" plural</b> all over the OT</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>more common in the Greek</b> than Hebrew</li><li class=\"d1\">more common the <b>later</b> in Jewish history</li></ul>"},{"t":1762.74,"lv":3,"h":"Gods plural and \"monotheism\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why it matters: 18th-19th c. <b>German scholars</b> - evolution from <b>polytheist → monotheist</b> Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">neither is true</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Monotheism</b> = idea from <b>late 16th / early 17th c.</b> - term doesn't exist before</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>monos</i> and <i>theos</i> Greek - but fathers never use \"monotheism\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they call pagans <b>polytheists</b>; for themselves: <i>monarchia</i> = <b>one first principle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one God unlike any other being called God - from <b>St. Paul</b>: \"many gods and many lords, but for us one God the Father and one Lord Jesus Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>: <b>Melchizedek scroll</b> - in the end times Melchizedek the Messiah comes with <b>all the righteous gods</b></li><li class=\"d1\">2nd-c. BC Jews, not early polytheists - different use of the word</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Gods\" = spiritual / divine beings: angels, demons (usually covered up in English)</li><li class=\"d0\">Ways to mark <b>Yahweh</b> as different: he <b>created</b> them, eternal; Greek uses the <b>article</b> (\"<b>the</b> God\") ~ capital G</li></ul>"},{"t":2003.89,"lv":3,"h":"What the serpent is really saying","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"eat the apple and you'll turn into <b>God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Saying: you'll be like <b>divine beings, like the serpent</b> - \"one of us instead of one of you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"God doesn't want that - that's why he told you not to\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The lie is a <b>half truth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they do in a way become like those beings</li><li class=\"d1\">serpent knows <b>good and evil</b> - more about the evil right now</li><li class=\"d0\">Adam and Eve don't - mark of their <b>innocence</b>, not perfection; innocent <b>like children</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isaiah</b> (Christmas services): before the child is old enough to know good and evil</li><li class=\"d1\">= coming to <b>maturity</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2097.17,"lv":3,"h":"The shortcut: a pattern of the demonic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Devil's offer: God created you; <b>here's the shortcut</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not dangerous to eat before ready - he doesn't want you to <b>do this adult thing as a kid</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pattern</b> in first <b>11 chapters</b> of Genesis, and beyond</li><li class=\"d1\">demonic beings, Satan offer <b>kinds of knowledge</b> before humanity is ready</li><li class=\"d1\">knowing humans will use it <b>destructively</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2171.72,"lv":2,"h":"3:6 - She ate, and gave to her husband","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:6</b>: tree <b>good for food, pleasant to the eyes, beautiful to contemplate</b>; took fruit and ate; gave to husband <b>with her</b></li><li class=\"d0\">(likes this translation)</li><li class=\"d0\">She says nothing to God - conversation \"as if God's off somewhere\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no clarification; tree and fruit look good; <b>seems right in her own eyes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gives it to Adam - who's <b>with her</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2234.65,"lv":2,"h":"Adam standing there","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Adam there <b>the whole conversation</b> - not serpent tricking her alone</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Letting this all unfold\" - \"let's watch her eat it and see what happens\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He should have <b>shut it down</b></li><li class=\"d1\">part of why he's there: <b>tend and protect</b> the garden</li><li class=\"d1\">creation followed the <b>temple</b> pattern (ANE); Adam = <b>priest</b></li><li class=\"d1\">should have <b>driven the serpent out</b> or told God</li><li class=\"d0\">Instead: when wife doesn't drop dead, he <b>disobeys too</b>, follows her lead</li></ul>"},{"t":2341.84,"lv":2,"h":"3:7 - Eyes opened, fig leaves","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:7</b>: eyes of both opened, knew they were <b>naked</b>; sewed <b>fig leaves</b> for coverings</li><li class=\"d0\">Created naked, <b>not ashamed</b> = <b>innocence</b> imagery of children</li><li class=\"d1\">fine for little kids to run around naked up to an age - then stops, and that's appropriate</li><li class=\"d0\">Eat → <b>innocence gone</b>; <b>shame</b> comes</li><li class=\"d0\">Kids' books: Adam and Eve behind bushes with convenient branches - now they need <b>sewn fig leaves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Immediate effect: <b>loss of innocence, loss of purity</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2426.3,"lv":3,"h":"Knowing evil: eating means taking in","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Leapfrogged, but in the wrong way\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Already knew <b>good</b> - God called everything good as he created</li><li class=\"d1\">except Adam, till wife made: \"not good that man should be alone\"; then good</li><li class=\"d0\">What they gained: <b>knowledge of evil</b> - of something other than good</li><li class=\"d0\">Knowledge not just <b>data</b>: <i>yada</i> = to know, used at end of chapter - \"<b>Adam knew his wife</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans: <b>knowing vs knowing about</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wife's old friends, her doctor's chart know <b>about</b> her - none know her as I do</li><li class=\"d0\">Eating from a tree: ancient view = <b>what you eat becomes part of you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not theoretical knowledge of what evil might be - <b>they came to know evil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not lost naivete: <b>good still in them, plus evil</b> now a part of them</li></ul>"},{"t":2617.39,"lv":2,"h":"3:8 - The voice of the Lord walking","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:8</b>: heard <b>voice of the Lord God walking</b> in the garden; hid within the tree from his presence</li><li class=\"d0\">Pretty literal: <b>Yahweh</b> God walking in the garden</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew: \"in the <b>heat</b> of the day\" - NIV \"cool of the day\" (\"what are you gonna do?\")</li><li class=\"d1\">literally <i>ruach hayom</i> = <b>spirit of the day</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>ruach</i> also wind → \"hot wind\" → heat; NIV guesses breeze</li></ul>"},{"t":2695.16,"lv":3,"h":"The Spirit of the day: day of the Lord","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Spirit\" matters - the <b>Spirit and presence of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>hayom</i> = <b>the day</b> - later in OT <b>the day of Yahweh</b>; carried into NT (\"that day\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Day of the Lord = <b>judgment, visitation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Yahweh comes and things <b>get sorted out</b>; judgment = <b>things put back in order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">visit \"not grandpa visiting for tea and scones\" - <b>\"wait till your dad gets home\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Resonance here: <b>injustice</b> has just happened, creation out of whack → sorting out coming</li></ul>"},{"t":2813.14,"lv":2,"h":"God has a body; the Memra","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Notice: first of many places in Torah where <b>God has a body</b></li><li class=\"d1\">walking, eating...</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tension</b>: \"no one can see God and live\" vs. people who <b>see God and live</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Exodus 33</b>: Moses speaks to God <b>face to face</b>; nine-ten verses later \"you can't see me and live\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Targums</b> (Aramaic translations, loose - \"the Message of the Hebrew Bible\")</li><li class=\"d1\">add Aramaic <i>memra</i> = <b>word</b> wherever God walks, eats, has hands</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Moses spoke to the <b>Memra of Yahweh</b> face to face\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second Temple Judaism</b>: <b>intermediary figure</b> between unseen Yahweh and creatures</li><li class=\"d1\">identified as Yahweh, but <b>can be seen</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Memra Yahweh</b> (Aramaic), <b>Dabar Yahweh</b> (Hebrew) = names for him</li><li class=\"d0\">Christians quickly combine all these → <b>Jesus</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3028.71,"lv":2,"h":"Justin Martyr and John's prologue","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Justin Martyr</b>, mid-2nd c.: Christ appeared in OT as <b>angel of the Lord, word of the Lord</b> - lists them all</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: behind the <b>prologue of John</b>? - yes</li><li class=\"d1\">\"in the beginning was the <b>Word</b>, and the Word was with God\" - Jewish thing, audience would know</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the Word became flesh\" = identifying <b>Jesus of Nazareth</b> as this figure</li><li class=\"d0\">So from 2nd c. on, fully appropriate to read: <b>Christ came walking in the garden</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God the Son, second person of the Trinity - however you identify him</li></ul>"},{"t":3109.87,"lv":3,"h":"Judgment given to the Son","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not disconnected from <b>judgment and the day</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John</b>: \"even the Father judges no one; <b>all judgment given to the Son</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't say \"from now on\" - <b>from the beginning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">On hearing him coming, rather than greeting him, they <b>go and hide</b></li></ul>"}]});
